    "narrative": "For Ghost Ride the WHIP\u2019s June 27th slate, our offensive ceiling is anchored by **James Wood** (76.28) and **Mickey Moniak** (75.98). **James Wood** presents our most compelling profile; batting lead-off against a favorable 4.76 xERA matchup, his elite 0.396 xwOBA combined with massive park and skill bonuses makes him an auto-start despite the presence of a high-efficiency catcher. Similarly, **Mickey Moniak** benefits from a massive 19% SP skill boost and a 14% platoon advantage against Paredes, cementing his status as a premier play. **Jose Caballero** also demands attention with a 66.07 score; his massive 13% platoon edge and the opposing pitcher's propensity for yielding stolen bases provide the exact secondary category juice we prioritize for our 5x5 Roto standing.\n\nWhile **Austin Riley** (44.49) pushed hard for inclusion, he remains on our bench due to a tough draw against Logan Webb, whose sub-3.50 xERA suppresses the upside of even our top-tier talent. The optimizer favored the immediate efficiency of **Kyle Karros** and **Zach McKinstry** due to superior SP matchups, relegating **Austin Riley** to the pine as a high-floor insurance option. We are proceeding with caution regarding **Dalton Rushing** (40.69) and **Cole Young** (44.73), both of whom are hovering near the floor of our starting criteria. **Dalton Rushing** faces a difficult L/L matchup that heavily penalizes his output, and **Cole Young** must contend with an elite-tier catcher hold rate that negates some of his baserunning upside. While no major weather alerts are currently flagged, the high SP skill of opposing starters facing **Max Muncy** and **Seiya Suzuki** requires close monitoring; their scores are precarious, and any late-breaking lineup shifts could necessitate a rapid re-evaluation of our utility spots to protect our season-long game caps.",
